{"sections":[],"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"declarations","declarations":[{"tokens":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"class"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"ProceduralSkyMaterial"}],"languages":["swift"],"platforms":["macOS"]}]},{"content":[{"type":"heading","text":"Overview","anchor":"overview","level":2},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/ProceduralSkyMaterial","isActive":true},{"text":" provides a way to create an effective background quickly by defining procedural parameters for the sun, the sky and the ground. The sky and ground are defined by a main color, a color at the horizon, and an easing curve to interpolate between them. Suns are described by a position in the sky, a color, and a max angle from the sun at which the easing curve ends. The max angle therefore defines the size of the sun in the sky.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/ProceduralSkyMaterial","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" supports up to 4 suns, using the color, and energy, direction, and angular distance of the first four "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/DirectionalLight3D","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" nodes in the scene. This means that the suns are defined individually by the properties of their corresponding "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/DirectionalLight3D","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":"s and globally by "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/ProceduralSkyMaterial\/sunAngleMax","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/ProceduralSkyMaterial\/sunCurve","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inl